Native daemon-runtime maturity gate
===================================
Build the static VM-only PID 1 fixture with:
  make -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu) -C /usr/src/tools/test/daemon-runtime all
Build libservice, BSDFilesystem and their tests from the same source state first.

run-vm.py requires --root (a NO_ROOT staged world), --objtop (the standard
/usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64), a new --work beneath /usr/obj, --qemu and
--qemu-data. It requires exact staged/object kernel hashes and matching kernel
configurations, and static test/fixture binaries. It copies inputs into its
own staging tree, with root ownership represented in a makefs METALOG.
No host root login, install, mount or reboot is needed. It does not write the
input staging root or attach another VM's writable disk. Compilation uses all
logical CPUs; this small runtime guest uses two CPUs and 1 GiB RAM.

The VM has no network and boots a PID 1 fixture instead of the capability plane.
It runs native session/bootstrap deadline and session lifecycle tests as root,
plus the BSDFilesystem descriptor-hardening failure regression.
A skip, missing/duplicate pass marker, timeout, panic, WITNESS reversal or
unclean shutdown fails the run. inputs.json and result.json retain input hashes
and the exact QEMU command; serial.log retains output, including failed runs.
This gate does not qualify booted daemons, actual OOM, ACPI suspend, hardware,
installer behavior, or a complete system image. Never run runtime_init as the
host's init. Source and object inputs must be coordinated before full acceptance.

Use --client-ownership to include the static audit, crypto, trace and log
client lifecycle suites. Build these libraries/tests first. The runner records
each binary hash and derives the required cases from its ATF listing; each
case gets its own directory and 40-second process timeout. No skip is a pass.
These suites exercise real descriptor operations with service test doubles;
they do not replace integrated daemon/provider tests.

Use --oes-factory to stage the current object tree's oes.ko and boot it in the
disposable guest. The runner hashes the module and static factory and handoff tests.
This exercises real OES NOTIFY descriptor attenuation after cap_enter, including
denial of AUTH subscriptions and mode/scope changes. It does not validate
SwitchBoard attribute admission. The handoff cases exercise real channel
descriptor delivery, denied onward transfer, malformed-request rejection, and
the five-second idle deadline.

The OES gate also checks factory quota boundaries and ordinary filesystem
mediation of a real intermediate-directory alias to /dev/oes. It proves the
alias resolves before mediation, is denied by the grant path, and an ordinary
/dev/null grant remains usable. The shipped manifest parser test is separate;
neither check substitutes for full SwitchBoard client admission.

Use --log-store to run every static BSDLog store_test case, including partial
write rollback, failed rollback admission, reopening recovery, privacy, scoped
queries, reclamation and retention. Tests use real files with injected write
and truncation errors; they do not qualify physical power-loss durability.
